"Think about what you ate today. Rice, bread, noodles, vegetables, eggs: nearly all of it came from a farm. Yet for most of the 300,000 years of our species, there were no farms anywhere. People gathered wild plants, fished, and hunted. Then, starting about 12,000 years ago, people in some places began to plant seeds and to keep animals. It was one of the biggest changes in human history. Why did it happen? The first surprise is that it happened more than once. Farming began independently in at least eleven regions of the world, among people who did not learn it from one another. In West Asia they grew wheat and barley. In China it was millet in the north and rice in the south. In the Americas it was squash and maize. In New Guinea it was bananas and taro, and in Africa sorghum. Each group worked with the wild plants that grew nearby. The second surprise is how slow it was. Nobody woke up one morning as a farmer. Wild wheat drops its seeds when they are ripe, which is good for the plant and bad for the person trying to collect them. A few plants hold on to their seeds. Most researchers think the change went like this: when people cut the grain and sowed some of it again, those were the seeds they carried home, so each year a few more plants were of that kind. For wheat, barley, and rice, it took about two to four thousand years before nearly all the plants kept their seeds. All that time, people went on gathering and hunting as well. Why did people begin? Researchers do not agree. Some point to the climate: the last ice age ended about 11,700 years ago, and the weather became warmer and steadier; only then, they argue, did farming become possible. Others think that growing numbers of people needed more food. Others note that some foragers were already living in villages before they farmed, or that people were gathering in large numbers for feasts. The reasons may well have been different in different places. Farming changed people's lives, and not only for the better. A field can feed more people than the same land left wild, and the number of people grew. But in most places that have been studied, skeletons show that people became shorter as farming took hold, a sign of poorer food or more sickness. Stored grain could be owned, and the gap between richer and poorer families widened. A farmer is also tied to the field. When a harvest failed, it was much harder to move away. Farming spread in two ways. Sometimes farmers moved into new lands and took their seeds and animals with them. Sometimes foragers learned from their neighbors. And some peoples kept other ways of getting food for thousands of years. About 5,000 years ago, people in Taiwan were growing both millet and rice. Today almost everything we eat is farmed, and few of us ever wonder who first tamed the plants on our plates.": "0bad4f038b95",
